<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Glywysing</h1><p class="st-meta">470 CE – 942 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of south-east Wales between the Usk and the Tawe, home of the great monasteries of Llantwit and Llancarfan, which became Morgannwg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Glywysing took its name from Glywys, a legendary 5th-century king whose descendants, according to the Lives of the Welsh saints, included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cadoc">St Cadoc</a>. It grew from the post-Roman communities of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-glamorgan">Glamorgan</a> lowlands, once the territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/silures">Silures</a>, and was often joined with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Kingdom of Gwent</a> under a single dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-llandaff">Llandaff charters</a>, a collection of land grants, show a society of kings, churchmen and landowners trading estates and gifts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meurig-ap-tewdrig">Meurig ap Tewdrig</a> and his descendants ruled across Glywysing and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Gwent</a> from the 7th century. The monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llantwit-major">Llantwit Major</a>, founded by St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illtud">Illtud</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cadoc">St Cadoc</a>, were leading centres of learning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a> says Hywel ap Rhys submitted to Alfred.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 10th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morgan-ab-owain">Morgan Hen</a> ruled both Glywysing and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Gwent</a>, and from around 942 his combined realm became known as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morgannwg">Morgannwg</a>, from which the name <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-glamorgan">Glamorgan</a> derives. The region later fell to the Normans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-fitzhamon">Robert Fitzhamon</a> in the 1090s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-glywysing.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Glywysing" width="320" height="421"><figcaption>Image: Owj20 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medieval_Wales.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>470 CE – 942 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meurig ap Tewdrig, Hywel ap Rhys, Morgan Hen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Glywysing</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=706&amp;focus=kingdom-of-glywysing" aria-label="Kingdom of Glywysing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4603 395 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Glywysing lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4923 651L4927 648"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4927 648h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4923 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4923 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=706&amp;focus=kingdom-of-glywysing">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
